L57: Listen to the Layers: Mitigating Hallucinations with Inter-Layer Disagreement
L58: 
L59: Koduvayur Subbalakshmi Sabbir Hossain Ujjal
L60: Venkata Krishna Teja Mangichetty Nastaran Jamalipour Soofi
L61: Stevens Institute of Technology, Hoboken, NJ, USA
L62: ksubbala@stevens.edu, sujjal@stevens.edu, vmangich@stevens.edu, njamalip@stevens.edu
L63: ###### Abstract
L64: Pretrained Large Language Models (LLMs) are prone to generating fluent yet factually incorrect text—a phenomenon known as hallucinations, undermining their reliability and utility in downstream tasks. We hypothesize that a generated text span’s factuality is correlated with its representational instability across the model’s internal layers.
L65: Based on this, we propose the CoCoA (Confusion and Consistency Aware) decoder, a novel, training-free decoding algorithm that mitigates hallucinations at inference time by listening to these signals in the middle layers. We propose two metrics to quantify this instability in the middle layers, and use it to penalize outputs that exhibit high internal confusion, thereby steering the model towards more internally consistent and factually grounded outputs.
L66: We further propose a self-information gated variant, CoCoA-SIG, that dynamically modulates this penalty to selectively target high-surprise, unstable generations. Extensive experiments on diverse tasks, including question-answering, summarization and code generation demonstrate that CoCoA significantly improves factual correctness across multiple model families (e.g., Llama-3, Qwen-2.5, Mistral).
L67: By leveraging model-intrinsic signals, CoCoA offers an effective and broadly applicable method for enhancing the trustworthiness of LLMs at inference time, without requiring any model retraining.
L68: ## 1 Introduction
L69: 
L70: cite35†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Comparison between Greedy decoding and CoCoA decoding for Llama-3-8b-Instruct. CoCoA decoding improves LLM’s reliability by generating truthful responses.
L71: Pretrained large language models (LLMs) have shown significant performance gains in downstream tasks  cite36†Achiam et al. [2023] ; cite37†Comanici et al. [2025] ; cite38†Grattafiori et al. [2024] ; cite39†Qwen et al. [2025] ; cite40†Rozière et al. [2024] . However, these models can generate fluent responses that are factually incorrect (see Fig. cite41†1 ) This phenomenon, known as hallucination, is a persistent challenge for large language models (LLMs)  cite42†Ferrando et al. [2025] ; cite43†Tang et al.
L72: [2025] ; cite44†Orgad et al. [2025] ; cite45†Li et al. [2024] ; cite46†Liang et al. [2024] and can undermine the reliability of downstream tasks relying on LLMs, especially in critical applications and agentic systems.
L73: Hallucination mitigation has been approached from several directions cite47†Manakul et al. [2023] ; cite48†Friel and Sanyal [2023] ; cite49†Chen et al. [2024] ; cite50†Su et al. [2024] ; cite51†Binkowski et al. [2025] ; cite52†Gema et al. [2024] ; cite53†Hu et al. [2024] ; cite43†Tang et al. [2025] ; cite54†Van der Poel et al. [2022] ; cite55†Lu et al. [2024] ; cite56†Chuang et al. [2024] . Strategies range from modifying the model’s core knowledge through training based approaches like specialized fine-tuning cite53†Hu et al.
L74: [2024] to altering the inference process itself. Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) based methods cite57†Fan et al. [2024] ; cite58†Shuster et al. [2021] ; cite59†Cheng et al. [2023] ; cite60†Gao et al. [2023] grounds the model output at inference time using external data, while post-hoc verification methods attempt to correct errors after a response has been generated.
L75: A third, complementary class of strategies build inference-time decoder methods cite52†Gema et al. [2024] ; cite56†Chuang et al. [2024] ; cite55†Lu et al. [2024] . Our solution falls in this third class, and is based on the core idea that a text span which is internally stable across the LLM’s processing layers is more likely to be coherent, grounded, and non-hallucinatory. Our approach is inspired by a growing body of work on mechanistic interpretability cite61†Wallat et al. [2020] ; cite62†Meng et al.
L76: [2022] ; cite63†Yu et al. [2023] ; cite64†Lv et al. [2024] ; cite65†Yu et al. [2024] that has shown that factual knowledge is not uniformly distributed throughout the model, but are primarily processed in the intermediate middle layers of the LLM. This crucial finding gives a specific region within the model to explore for signals for factual recall.
L77: Building on this prior work, we hypothesize that if the middle layers function as the factual information processing unit, then the stability of representation within this region should correlate with the factuality of the output. We posit that the successful recall of a fact will manifest as a stable and consistent representation as it is processed through these middle layers.
L78: On the other hand, if the facts are not recalled well in these layers, resulting in a hallucination, then there must be representational instability and semantic disagrement in these layers.This interlayer disagreement can therefore serve as a model-intrinsic signal for hallucination mitigation. Our contributions are as follows:
L79:   * •
L80: 
L81: We propose two metrics (ConMLDS , and fMLDS ) to quantify representational instability.
L82: 
L83:   * •
L84: 
L85: We propose a training-free decoder (CoCoA ) to mitigate hallucinations using these metrics to steer the model towards outputs that exhibit higher internal consistency.
L86: 
L87:   * •
L88: 
L89: We introduce a self-information gated variant, ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ , which dynamically modulates the penalty to selectively target high-surprise, unstable generations.
L90: Extensive experiments on diverse tasks across multiple model families (e.g., Llama-3, Mistral, Qwen) and sizes demonstrate that CoCoA and ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ significantly improves factual correctness, consistently outperforming strong inference-time baselines. By directly probing the layers responsible for factual processing the proposed decoders provide a broadly applicable solution to enhance LLM trustworthiness without any training.
L91: ## 2 Related Work
L92: Consistency checks on multiple responses to the same query has been used to detect hallucination cite49†Chen et al. [2024] ; cite47†Manakul et al. [2023] ; cite48†Friel and Sanyal [2023] .  cite49†Chen et al. [2024] propose a white-box strategy based on an Eigen score computed from the covariance matrix of sentence embeddings of the answers.  cite47†Manakul et al.
L93: [2023] ; cite48†Friel and Sanyal [2023] are black-box techniques where the consistency between the responses is measured through a powerful LLM as a judge, or other consistency metrics calculated on the generated responses.
L94: Mitigation methods can be categorized based on their operational paradigm and the signals used. The more resource intensive approaches seek to enhance factual grounding in the training process and includes methods like fine-tuning  cite53†Hu et al. [2024] , and reinforcement learning with human feedback  cite66†Lin et al. [2025] ; cite67†Wei et al. [2025] . Targeted knowledge editing in another strategy where model weights are surgically altered to reduce hallucinations cite62†Meng et al. [2022] .
L95: Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) methods  cite57†Fan et al. [2024] ; cite58†Shuster et al. [2021] ; cite59†Cheng et al. [2023] ; cite60†Gao et al. [2023] rely on external knowledge sources to control hallucinations, and are distinct from post-hoc methods that fact-check and revise outputs after they are generated.
L96: The third class of techniques work at inference time cite56†Chuang et al. [2024] ; cite55†Lu et al. [2024] ; cite52†Gema et al. [2024] , by analyzing the model’s internal state. Traditionally, such methods have focused on uncertainty quantification(UQ), which estimates the reliability of the model’s final output. These methods often rely on statistical techniques, such as measuring the variance across a deep ensemble cite68†Lakshminarayanan et al.
L97: [2017] or using Monte Carlo Dropout cite69†Gal and Ghahramani [2016] , to derive a confidence score and often treat the model as a black box.
L98: More recently, inspired by work in mechanistic interpretability, several “white-box” methods have emerged. These methods hypothesize that a model’s uncertainty or confusion leaves a detectable trace within its internal computational process. The seminal work by cite70†Azaria and Mitchell [2023] showed that the model’s internal states can reveal when it is “lying”. More recently cite71†Ji et al. [2024] show that hallucination risk can be predicted by the query alone.
L99: Activation steering methods like cite72†Wang et al. [2025] actively edits hidden states during forward pass to steer them towards a pre-learned “truthful direction”, by training an auxillary model.
L100: A different approach is to measure a property of the model’s internal state and use it as a metric to guide the decoding, without editing the model. Our work belongs to this paradigm, and differs from the other methods in this category in how we identify the internal signals of confusion and how we use it:
L101: 
L102:   * •
L103: Contrastive decoders generate a signal by contrasting different model states. cite56†Chuang et al. [2024] contrasts the logit predictions of a “mature” final layer with those of a “premature” earlier layer,and cite52†Gema et al. [2024] contrasts the full model against a degraded version of it.
L104: 
L105:   * •
L106: 
L107: cite55†Lu et al. [2024] uses the mutual information between the input and the candidate spans as a signal.
L108: Our work, builds on the seminal work by cite70†Azaria and Mitchell [2023] , and quantifies the representational instability of a candidate span as it evolves through the model’s middle layers. It does not rely on model contrasts, model editing, or the mutual information between the context and the spans. By doing this, CoCoA is a truly training-free, self-contained, inference time decoder, distinctly different from the state-of-the-art.
L109: ## 3 CoCoA: Confusion and Consistency Aware Decoder
L110: 
L111: As mentioned earlier, our work is based on the the hypothesis that a response that shows instability in the middle layers of the LLM is likely to be hallucinated. We first quantify this confusion, and then design a decoder to mitigate hallucination.
L112: ### 3.1 Measuring the Disagreement in the Middle Layers
L113: We propose two methods to quantify the representational instability of the candidate span as it passes through the middle layers of the LLM. For each candidate span $S=(y_{p},y_{p+1},...,y_{q})$, where $y_{p}$ is the first token of the span and $y_{q}$ is the last, we extract the hidden state vectors, $h_{i,l}\in\mathbf{R}^{d}$, for token $y_{i}$ at layer $l$, $\forall i=p...q$ and $l=m,\cdots n$, where $m$ is the first of the middle layers and $n$ is the last of the middle layers.
L114: We use mean pooling to capture the average semantic content. Let $H_{S,l}$ be the aggregated span representation for the $l^{\rm th}$ layer. Then,
L115:  | $$H_{S,l}={\rm MeanPool}(\{h_{i,l}\mid i\in[p,q]\})=\frac{1}{|S|}\sum_{i=p}^{q}h_{i,l}$$  |
L116: 
L117: We define the disagreement between any two layers $L_{a}$ and $L_{b}$, as the cosine distance between the two layers. So, ${\rm disagreement}(L_{a},L_{b})=1-S_{C}(H_{S,L_{a}},H_{S,L_{b}})$, where $S_{C}(H_{S,L_{a}},H_{S,L_{b}})=\frac{H_{S,L_{a}}\cdot H_{S,L_{b}}}{||H_{S,L_{a}}||\cdot||H_{S,L_{b}}||}$ and is the cosine similarity between the two representation vectors.
L118: #### 3.1.1 Consecutive Middle Layer Disagreement Score
L119: 
L120: We then define a consecutive middle layer disagreement score (ConMLDS ) as:
L121: 
L122:  | $${\rm conMLDS}(S)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=m}^{n-1}(1-S_{C}((H_{S,j},H_{S,j+1})),$$  |  | (1)
L123: where $m$ is the first middle layer, $n$ is the last of the middle layers, $L$ is the final layer and $N=n-m+1$ is the number of middle layers. This metric accumulates the differences in the representations between consecutive intermediate layers of the LLM. We choose this over, all-pair difference to keep the complexity smaller. Larger discordance between the representations in the middle layers will result in a larger value of ConMLDS .
L124: #### 3.1.2 Relative Middle Layer Disagreement Score
L125: 
L126: We propose a second way to measure the confusion in the middle layers by comparing the representation vectors at each of the middle layers with the final layer. This method uses the representation at the final layer as a reference point to compute the disagreement score.
L127: 
L128:  | $${\rm fMLDS}(S)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=m}^{n}(1-S_{C}((H_{S,j},H_{S,L})),$$  |  | (2)
L129: 
L130: We hypothesize that confusion in the middle layers would lead to higher fMLDS .
L131: ### 3.2 The Proposed CoCoA Decoder
L132: 
L133: With the metrics proposed in Eqns cite73†1 , and cite74†2 , we propose decoders that mitigate hallucination. The key design principle is that hallucination is an instance-specific failure. A successful decoder cannot apply a uniform, “average” correction; it must dynamically assess and re-rank candidate spans at each generation step based on their real-time confusion scores.
L134: We first propose a composite metric that integrates the middle layer disagreement score (ConMLDS or fMLDS ) into the standard auto-regressive decoding process. In this subsection, we use the abbreviation “MLDS” to mean ConMLDS or fMLDS , depending on which variant is incorporated into the decoder, since the discussion is the same for both.
L135: The standard greedy decoder chooses the most probable next output token $y_{i}$, given the input $x_{1},x_{2},\ldots x_{n}$, and the distribution at each decoding step. Hence for the standard decoder, $y_{i}\sim\log p(y_{i}|y<i,x)$. This approach can create hallucinations cite75†Rawte et al. [2023] ; cite76†Ji et al. [2023] ; cite77†Huang et al. [2025] .
L136: We modify this process in two ways. Firstly, we generate spans of tokens at a time, to provide better context to the decoding process. Secondly, we penalize spans that demonstrate more confusion in the middle layers by incorporating the MLDS from Eqn cite73†1 or cite74†2 . In order to guide the LLM towards outputs that demonstrate less confusion in the middle layers, we propose a confusion and consistency aware (CoCoA ) decoder, and a self information gated variant of it, ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ decoder.
L137: cite78†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Overview of the proposed CoCoA decoding framework. At each decoding step (1) The LLM generates multiple candidate spans. (2) For each candidate span, we extract hidden state representations from selected middle layers.
L138: (3) For each span, we compute the Middle Layer Disagreement Score (MLDS), which quantifies representational inconsistency across layers as proposed in Eqns cite73†1 and cite74†2 (4) The CoCoA decoder combines forward log-probability with MLDS to produce a unified score, and finally, the span with the highest combined score—corresponding to low middle-layer confusion and high consistency—is selected as the output.
L139: In the CoCoA decoder, to penalize the spans that exhibit confusion in the middle layers, we subtract a weighted MLDS from the log probability of the span: $\log p_{S}-\alpha*{\rm MLDS}(S)$, where $\alpha$ is the weighting factor for the penalty term. When ${\rm MLDS}(S)$ is high (denoting high confusion in the middle layers), the above metric penalizes the span more. The CoCoA decoder will select the candidate span, $y_{i:i+k-1}$, that maximizes the CoCoA metric. Hence, for the CoCoA decoder,
L140:  | $$y_{i:i+k+1}\sim\log p_{S}-\alpha*{\rm MLDS}(S),$$  |  | (3)
L141: 
L142: Fig. cite79†2 provides an overview of the CoCoA decoder.
L143: #### 3.2.1 Self-Information Gating
L144: 
L145: The vanilla CoCoA decoder, represented by Eqn. cite80†3 , penalizes all spans in accordance with their MLDS scores only. Next we refine this metric by scaling the penalty term with the self information of the candidate span. That is, we modify the above penalty term to
L146:  | $\displaystyle{\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}(S)$  | $\displaystyle=$  |
L147:  |  | $\displaystyle\log p_{S}-(-\log(p_{S})*\alpha{\rm MLDS}(S))$  |
L148:  | $\displaystyle{=}$  | $\displaystyle\log p_{S}[1+\alpha*{\rm MLDS}(S)]$  |
L149: This effectively increases the weight assigned to the internal confusion in the model, for spans that are less likely, and therefore, whose self-information (or surprise factor) is higher. The ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ decoder will select the candidate span, $y_{i:i+k-1}$, that maximizes the ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ metric. Hence, for the ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ decoder,
L150: 
L151:  | $$y_{i:i+k+1}\sim\log p_{S}[1+\alpha*{\rm MLDS}(S)],$$  |  | (4)
L152: Using self-information gating allows us to modulate the penalty in such a way that it penalizes the less likely spans more and does not aggressively intervene in higher probability spans. Since hallucination likely occurs more at the edge of the internal knowledge limit of the LLMs, self-information gating should work better at managing the penalty in these regimes.
L153: #### 3.2.2 Risk Points
L154: 
L155: Prior research has identified that the tokens predicted with high confidence are typically less prone to error cite81†Guo et al. [2017] ; cite82†Zhu et al. [2023] . cite83†Li et al. [2023] proposed an approach to detect the positions that might lead to inaccurate decoding. These high risk points, called the divergence points, are identified Divergence points are identified using the method similar to that proposed by cite83†Li et al. [2023] , and also used by cite55†Lu et al. [2024] .
L156: As in cite83†Li et al. [2023] and cite55†Lu et al. [2024] , we first calculate the set ${\cal C}_{i}$ using
L157: 
L158:  | $${\cal C}_{i}=y_{i}\in{\cal V}|p(y_{i}|y<i)\geq\gamma\max_{w\in{\cal V}}p(w|y_{i}),$$  |  | (5)
L159: where ${\cal V}$ is the vocabulary and $\gamma$ is a hyperparameter that controls the range. This essentially is a set of tokens for which the probability, given the context, is greater than $\gamma$ times the maximum probability token in the vocabulary at that point. If $|{\cal C}_{i}|>1$, the point is deemed as a divergence point, and for each candidate token in ${\cal C}_{i}$, the LLM is made to continue generating tokens to create spans. Note that, unlike cite55†Lu et al.
L160: [2024] , we do not modify the distribution of the vocabulary before generating candidate spans. However, inspired by cite55†Lu et al. [2024] , we allow the decoder to generate spans of variable length at divergence points. We use the same procedure to generate the variable length spans as in cite55†Lu et al. [2024] , with the key difference that we do not use the point-wise mutual information described in cite55†Lu et al. [2024] in our metric, and do not change the distribution.
L161: Note also, that because we do not include point-wise mutual information in our metric, we do not need to employ teacher-forcing in our decoder implementation, which reduces the complexity significantly. We apply the CoCoA decoding (and the ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ variant) selectively only at these divergence points, and default to the standard greedy decoding elsewhere.
L162: Figure 3: Visualization of the effect of the CoCoA and ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ decoder on span level decisions. The x-axis represents different candidate spans, and y-axis shows their fMLDS score. Our decoder moves the decision towards the non-hallucinated output as shown by the arrows.
L163: Fig. cite84†3 visualizes the workings of the CoCoA , and ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ decoders for seven different divergence points. At each of these points, the different spans are characterized by different values of the fMLDS score. While the standard greedy decoder picks a plausible, but highly confused span, the proposed decoders nudge the decision to a span that scores less on the confusion index. The magnitude and the direction of the nudge is individualized to the particular decoding instance.
L164: ## 4 Experimental Setup
L165: 
L166: We evaluate CoCoA across multiple benchmarks to assess its effectiveness in hallucination mitigation across diverse tasks.
L167: ##### Datasets:
L168: We use TruthfulQA cite85†Lin et al. [2022] and Natural Questions (NQ) cite86†Kwiatkowski et al. [2019] benchmark datasets for factual verification. TruthfulQA dataset contains 817 questions with corresponding multiple correct answers, a best answer, and multiple incorrect answers. For our experiments we use all 817 samples and evaluated them in both the generation task and the multiple-choice task. We employ two NQ variants: NQ cite86†Kwiatkowski et al. [2019] and NQ-Swap cite87†Longpre et al. [2021] .
L169: NQ contains real user queries with context and annotated answers, while NQ-Swap presents adversarially modified questions designed to elicit hallucinated responses cite87†Longpre et al. [2021] . For our experiments we randomly selected 1000 samples from each datasets. We evaluate the decoder for summarization on two datasets: SAMSum cite88†Gliwa et al. [2019] , a dialogue summarization dataset containing human conversation and expert summaries, and XSum cite89†Narayan et al.
L170: [2018] , a collection of BBC news articles with abstractive summaries. For our experiments we randomly sampled 1000 samples from both SAMSum and XSum. Currently LLM are widely being used for code generation task, which requires complex understanding and reasoning. We utilize MBPP cite90†Austin et al. [2021] benchmark, containing 427 Python programming challenges with test cases for coding tasks.
L171: ##### Models:
L172: We evaluate the performance of CoCoA on multiple model families and scales to demonstrate its generalizability. We utilize Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct cite38†Grattafiori et al. [2024] , Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3 cite91†Jiang et al. [2023] , Qwen2.5-14B cite39†Qwen et al. [2025] , Qwen2.5-32B cite39†Qwen et al. [2025] , CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf cite40†Rozière et al. [2024] . (Code models are used for code generation tasks, and the rest of the models are used for all other tasks).
L173: We denote these models as Llama-3-8b, Mistral-7B, Qwen2.5-14B, Qwen2.5-32B, CodeLlama-7b.
L174: ##### Baselines:
L175: We compare CoCoA with four baselines. 1) Greedy decoding: The standard autoregressive decoding. 2) DoLa cite56†Chuang et al. [2024] : Decoding by contrasting layers, which modifies the logits based on the differences between final and early layer predictions to improve factuality. We utilize DoLa-high (contrasting the second half of the layers with the final layer) of DoLa variants throughout our experiments. 3) DeCoRe cite52†Gema et al.
L176: [2024] : A contrastive decoding approach that compares predictions from the original base model against a deliberately degraded “masked” version (with retrieval heads disabled to impair factual knowledge access), dynamically amplifying their differences to favor more factually grounded outputs. We use the DeCoRe_entropy variant. 4) Diver cite55†Lu et al. [2024] : A decoding strategy that uses mutual information verification to generate a factually grounded response.
L177: Additional details for implementation are available in Appendix cite26†A .
L178: Middle Layer Selection: Based on prior work  cite61†Wallat et al. [2020] ; cite62†Meng et al. [2022] ; cite63†Yu et al. [2023] ; cite64†Lv et al. [2024] ; cite65†Yu et al. [2024] showing factual information concentration in intermediate layers, we define middle layers as layers $m$ through $n$, where $m=\lfloor L/3\rfloor$ and $n=\lfloor 2L/3\rfloor$ for a model with $L$ total layers. Specific layer ranges are determined for each model architecture.
L179: Table cite92†6 in Sec. cite20†5 showing the performance for different middle layer sizes for Llama Llama-3-8b on TruthfulQA, suggests that this range works best in terms of size of middle layers.
L180: ##### Evaluation Metrics:
L181: For open-ended generation task on TruthfulQA, we followed the standard practice and evaluate using Truthfulness (%Truth), Informativeness (%Info), and $T\times I$  cite85†Lin et al. [2022] . We also calculated the rejection rate (i.e., the percentage of responses where the model answers with “I have no comment”). In some cases, this is the correct response (Table  cite93†12 , Appendix, cite32†C.4 ), and in others, it is not (Table cite94†13 ,Appendix. cite32†C.4 ).
L182: For truthfulness and informativeness scores, we evaluate responses using Gemini-2.5-Pro cite37†Comanici et al. [2025] . Detailed prompts for these evaluations are available in Appendix cite29†C.1 . We also report evaluation metrics calculated only on non-rejection answers. For multiple-choice evaluation on TruthfulQA, we report MC1, MC2, and MC3 metrics following the standard practice cite85†Lin et al. [2022] . For NQ and NQ-Swap we adopt exact match (EM) and F1 cite95†Rajpurkar et al.
L183: [2016] metrics for factuality evaluation. As LLMs tends to generate longer sequence, EM metric may misinterpret the overall quality of the generation for faithfulness. So, we also evaluate truthfulness for NQ & NQ-Swap using LLM-as-judge annotator. For summarization tasks we evaluate truthfulness along with FActScore  cite96†Min et al. [2023] and ROUGE-L cite97†Lin [2004] . Truthfulness and FActScore of the summary are evaluated using Gemini-2.5-Pro  cite37†Comanici et al.
L184: [2025] following the prompt described in Appendix  cite30†C.2 . For code evaluation, we use Pass@1 metric cite98†Chen [2021] . For all of these metrics, a higher score is better except for rejection rate.
